1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,875
Syed Mohamed Alsagoff and the
Estate That Printed Its Own Money

2
00:00:04,875 --> 00:00:08,639
On the 1st of May 1878,
a press in Singapore

3
00:00:08,719 --> 00:00:13,175
printed a set of banknotes
that no bank had issued.

4
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:17,708
They were worth 25 cents, 50
cents, one dollar and two dollars,

5
00:00:17,788 --> 00:00:22,475
they were printed on one side
only, and each was signed by hand.

6
00:00:22,475 --> 00:00:25,264
They were meant to be spent in one place,

7
00:00:25,344 --> 00:00:29,882
the estates of an Arab merchant
named Syed Mohamed Alsagoff on the

8
00:00:29,962 --> 00:00:33,600
island of Kukup, off the
south-western tip of Johor.

9
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:36,623
The Sultan of Johor
had given him the land,

10
00:00:36,703 --> 00:00:39,950
and with it the right
to issue his own money.

11
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:43,979
Syed Mohamed was born in
Singapore in 1836, into one of

12
00:00:44,059 --> 00:00:48,461
the Arab families from the
Hadhramaut, in what is now Yemen,

13
00:00:48,541 --> 00:00:52,425
who had settled across the
ports of the Malay world.

14
00:00:52,425 --> 00:00:55,556
His grandfather, Syed
Abdulrahman Alsagoff,

15
00:00:55,636 --> 00:00:59,962
had traded between Malacca and
Java in his own ships before

16
00:01:00,042 --> 00:01:03,925
coming to Singapore in the
settlement's early years.

17
00:01:03,925 --> 00:01:06,799
His father, Syed Ahmed, married Raja Siti,

18
00:01:06,879 --> 00:01:08,979
the daughter of Hajjah Fatimah,

19
00:01:09,059 --> 00:01:13,700
the Malacca-born merchant whose
mosque still stands on Beach Road.

20
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:16,900
I wrote about her in an earlier post.

21
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:20,877
Syed Ahmed founded the
trading firm of Alsagoff and

22
00:01:20,957 --> 00:01:25,650
Company in 1848 and took over
his mother-in-law's business.

23
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:28,935
When he died he was, in
the words of the Singapore

24
00:01:29,015 --> 00:01:32,098
chronicler Charles Burton
Buckley, "a very rich

25
00:01:32,178 --> 00:01:35,475
man," with steamers and
sailing ships of his own.

26
00:01:35,475 --> 00:01:40,785
The firm also carried Muslim pilgrims
between Southeast Asia and Jeddah,

27
00:01:40,865 --> 00:01:44,528
the port for Mecca, and
by 1871 the Alsagoff-owned

28
00:01:44,608 --> 00:01:49,175
Singapore Steamship Company was
taking them there by steamer.

29
00:01:49,175 --> 00:01:52,593
Syed Mohamed inherited
the firm, the ships and

30
00:01:52,673 --> 00:01:56,400
the pilgrim trade, and
became its senior partner.

31
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:01,383
Johor's ruler, Abu Bakar, who
took the title of Sultan in 1885,

32
00:02:01,462 --> 00:02:05,320
was opening his state to
planters from Singapore,

33
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:08,775
and he was a close patron of Syed Mohamed.

34
00:02:08,775 --> 00:02:12,840
In the late 1870s he granted
him land on Pulau Kukup,

35
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:17,767
a mangrove island at the southern
end of the Strait of Malacca,

36
00:02:17,847 --> 00:02:20,975
and later more land on the coast nearby.

37
00:02:20,975 --> 00:02:24,425
The estate was named Constantinople.

38
00:02:24,425 --> 00:02:28,387
By 1908 it grew rubber,
sago, coconuts, coffee,

39
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:33,025
cocoa and pineapples, and
about 200 men worked there.

40
00:02:33,025 --> 00:02:36,123
The Johor government
also gave him the right

41
00:02:36,203 --> 00:02:39,525
to issue private money
for use on his estates.

42
00:02:39,525 --> 00:02:43,130
The notes were printed
by the Al-Sa'aidi Press

43
00:02:43,210 --> 00:02:46,975
in Singapore and are
dated the 1st of May 1878.

44
00:02:46,975 --> 00:02:50,375
The ones that survive are very rare.

45
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:54,578
They carry hand-written signatures
and stamped serial numbers,

46
00:02:54,658 --> 00:02:56,581
and they were never listed in

47
00:02:56,661 --> 00:02:59,700
the standard world
catalogue of paper money.

48
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,566
The National Museum of
Singapore holds examples

49
00:03:03,646 --> 00:03:07,425
of the 25-cent, 50-cent
and one-dollar notes.

50
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:09,156
The estate needed labour,

51
00:03:09,236 --> 00:03:12,350
and some of it came
from the pilgrim trade.

52
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:16,560
Many pilgrims from the Dutch
East Indies ended up in Jeddah

53
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:21,075
destitute and in debt, with
nothing left for the voyage home.

54
00:03:21,075 --> 00:03:24,067
As the arrangement was
described at the time,

55
00:03:24,147 --> 00:03:27,958
an agent of Syed Mohamed's in
Jeddah paid their debts and

56
00:03:28,038 --> 00:03:31,577
their passage to Singapore,
and in return they signed

57
00:03:31,657 --> 00:03:35,275
contracts to work for him
until the money was repaid.

58
00:03:35,275 --> 00:03:38,130
In July 1881 the arrangement came

59
00:03:38,210 --> 00:03:41,500
before the Police Court in Singapore.

60
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:44,472
Fifty-two returned pilgrims from Semarang,

61
00:03:44,552 --> 00:03:47,816
in Java, were charged
by Syed Mohamed's coolie

62
00:03:47,896 --> 00:03:51,675
superintendent with breaking
their labour contracts.

63
00:03:51,675 --> 00:03:53,965
Their spokesman, Hadji Daoud,

64
00:03:54,045 --> 00:03:58,050
said they had agreed only
to repay a fare of $40.

65
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:01,985
In Singapore, he said, they were
locked in a house for two days,

66
00:04:02,065 --> 00:04:04,620
made to put their mark on a paper that was

67
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:07,883
not read to them, and set
to work on a plantation at

68
00:04:07,963 --> 00:04:11,225
Mount Pleasant for three
and a half dollars a month.

69
00:04:11,225 --> 00:04:14,350
When they were told to
go to Kukup they refused,

70
00:04:14,430 --> 00:04:16,500
and they said they were beaten.

71
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:19,787
They ran away to work on
another estate at Siglap,

72
00:04:19,867 --> 00:04:21,550
where they were arrested.

73
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:23,726
Their lawyer argued that the papers,

74
00:04:23,806 --> 00:04:27,549
which made the whole group liable
for the debt of any man who

75
00:04:27,629 --> 00:04:30,700
ran away or died, turned
them into "debt-slaves."

76
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:34,309
The magistrate found that
the contracts had been broken

77
00:04:34,389 --> 00:04:37,675
but referred a point of
law to the Supreme Court.

78
00:04:37,675 --> 00:04:40,793
The paper noted that,
under a new rule in Jeddah,

79
00:04:40,873 --> 00:04:45,818
pilgrims would only be allowed to land
there if they could support themselves

80
00:04:45,898 --> 00:04:50,075
and had money deposited at their
consulate for the journey home.

81
00:04:50,075 --> 00:04:52,871
A few days later the newspaper that had

82
00:04:52,951 --> 00:04:55,975
reported the case printed the other side.

83
00:04:55,975 --> 00:05:00,248
It said the Dutch consul in
Jeddah had advised such contracts,

84
00:05:00,328 --> 00:05:04,050
because the stranded pilgrims
would otherwise starve.

85
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:07,933
It said Syed Mohamed had paid
off their debts, that no man

86
00:05:08,013 --> 00:05:11,896
was sent to Kukup against his
will, and that officials who

87
00:05:11,976 --> 00:05:16,075
went to inspect the estate found
the labourers well treated.

88
00:05:16,075 --> 00:05:20,542
The paper said it was glad to clear
him of "the least suspicion of

89
00:05:20,622 --> 00:05:25,100
coercion," and that its criticism
had been aimed at "the system."

90
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:30,094
A century later, in a 1984 lecture,
the historian J. M. Gullick

91
00:05:30,174 --> 00:05:35,650
described the pilgrim contracts on
Kukup as debt bondage come again.

92
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:40,325
In Singapore, Syed Mohamed's
wealth was mostly in land.

93
00:05:40,325 --> 00:05:44,897
The family firm owned the Raffles
Hotel buildings on Beach Road,

94
00:05:44,977 --> 00:05:49,550
and the Perseverance Estate at
Geylang, more than 1,000 acres of

95
00:05:49,630 --> 00:05:54,500
citronella and lemon grass, with
a factory that distilled the oils.

96
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:58,452
He lived in a large house at
Bukit Tunggal, off Thomson Road,

97
00:05:58,532 --> 00:06:02,286
which the Straits Times called
a "magnificent palace," and

98
00:06:02,366 --> 00:06:06,200
he often travelled to Europe
and to Jeddah for his health.

99
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,900
He was also a public figure.

100
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:13,438
The Ottoman government made him its
consul in Singapore and gave him

101
00:06:13,518 --> 00:06:17,852
the Order of the Osmanieh, and
historians of Aceh record that the

102
00:06:17,932 --> 00:06:22,550
Acehnese asked him to act for them
in their long war with the Dutch.

103
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:27,200
The Sultan of Johor gave him
medals, a sword and a title.

104
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:30,654
In 1890 he built the
domed tomb over the grave

105
00:06:30,734 --> 00:06:34,802
of Habib Noh, a revered
Muslim saint, on Mount Palmer,

106
00:06:34,882 --> 00:06:37,725
which is still a place of pilgrimage.

107
00:06:37,725 --> 00:06:40,547
He gave to public appeals such as the

108
00:06:40,627 --> 00:06:43,450
fund for the Victoria Memorial Hall.

109
00:06:43,450 --> 00:06:47,082
Syed Mohamed died at Bukit Tunggal on the

110
00:06:47,162 --> 00:06:50,875
morning of the 3rd of July 1906, aged 70.

111
00:06:50,875 --> 00:06:55,125
The Straits Times called him
the head of the Arab community.

112
00:06:55,125 --> 00:06:58,808
His body lay in state at
the house, where Europeans,

113
00:06:58,888 --> 00:07:03,375
Muslims and the officials of
Johor came to pay their respects.

114
00:07:03,375 --> 00:07:07,288
That afternoon it was carried
on a bier, with the sword,

115
00:07:07,368 --> 00:07:11,567
medals and decorations from the
Sultan of Johor laid on top,

116
00:07:11,647 --> 00:07:16,425
under a yellow silk canopy, to his
family's mosque in Kampong Glam.

117
00:07:16,425 --> 00:07:20,800
A large crowd of Arabs and
Malays walked with it, chanting.

118
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:25,546
He was buried in the marble-floored
annexe of Hajjah Fatimah's mosque,

119
00:07:25,626 --> 00:07:27,625
beside his father and mother.

120
00:07:27,625 --> 00:07:30,935
As the mourners left the
mosque, the family gave

121
00:07:31,015 --> 00:07:34,475
every Muslim there a
dollar, rich and poor alike.

122
00:07:34,475 --> 00:07:38,019
All the public offices in
Johor closed for the day,

123
00:07:38,099 --> 00:07:41,429
the flag at the Raffles
Hotel flew at half-mast,

124
00:07:41,509 --> 00:07:45,275
and his firm's office in
Battery Road shut its doors.

125
00:07:45,275 --> 00:07:49,425
The firm passed to his
nephew, Syed Omar Alsagoff.

126
00:07:49,425 --> 00:07:53,742
According to the Malaysian writer
Faisal Tehrani, part of the

127
00:07:53,822 --> 00:07:58,715
Kukup land was sold to a planting
syndicate in the year of his death,

128
00:07:58,795 --> 00:08:02,975
and in 1926 the land on the
island went back to the state.

129
00:08:02,975 --> 00:08:05,758
Pulau Kukup is now a national park,

130
00:08:05,838 --> 00:08:10,501
and since 2003 it has been
protected as one of the world's

131
00:08:10,581 --> 00:08:14,425
important wetlands under
the Ramsar Convention.

132
00:08:14,425 --> 00:08:18,332
His will set up a charitable
trust, the SMA Wakaf,

133
00:08:18,412 --> 00:08:21,921
a wakaf being an endowment
held for religious

134
00:08:22,001 --> 00:08:25,350
and charitable purposes under Islamic law.

135
00:08:25,350 --> 00:08:29,496
In 1912 the trust opened
the Alsagoff Arab School

136
00:08:29,576 --> 00:08:33,975
on Jalan Sultan, in the
Kampong Glam he grew up in.

137
00:08:33,975 --> 00:08:37,835
It is the oldest madrasah
still standing in Singapore,

138
00:08:37,915 --> 00:08:40,250
and the trust still supports it.

139
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:43,995
The Alsagoffs were one of
several Hadhrami families

140
00:08:44,075 --> 00:08:47,825
who became great landowners
in colonial Singapore.

141
00:08:47,825 --> 00:08:51,011
The Aljunieds and the
Alkaffs were two others,

142
00:08:51,091 --> 00:08:53,639
and their names are still on the map,

143
00:08:53,719 --> 00:08:58,050
on Aljunied Road and at Alkaff
Mansion on Telok Blangah Hill.

144
00:08:58,050 --> 00:09:01,699
When I was courting my wife,
I lived off Jalan Kayu,

145
00:09:01,779 --> 00:09:05,284
and seeing her home meant
two buses, with a change

146
00:09:05,364 --> 00:09:08,950
at the corner of Serangoon
Road and Aljunied Road.

147
00:09:08,950 --> 00:09:13,027
It never occurred to me that
Aljunied was a real person,

148
00:09:13,107 --> 00:09:16,225
let alone the name of an immigrant family.

149
00:09:16,225 --> 00:09:21,225
When I was working at Security
Pacific, I dined at Alkaff Mansion.

150
00:09:21,225 --> 00:09:23,831
It was a slightly more expensive place,

151
00:09:23,911 --> 00:09:27,975
and brokers loved a chance to
spend money on their clients.

152
00:09:27,975 --> 00:09:32,775
It was cooler up there than down
in town, because it sat higher up.

153
00:09:32,775 --> 00:09:35,851
I have wanted to go back,
but it is out of the

154
00:09:35,931 --> 00:09:38,950
way now that I no longer
drive in Singapore.

155
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:43,177
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Syed Mohamed Alsagoff's life

156
00:09:43,257 --> 00:09:48,100
shows how much of colonial Singapore's
wealth was built by Arab merchant

157
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:52,350
families whose names few people
now connect with real people.

158
00:09:52,350 --> 00:09:57,021
His estate notes are a small,
rare record of how far a planter's

159
00:09:57,101 --> 00:10:01,772
power could reach in 19th-century
Johor, and the 1881 case shows

160
00:10:01,852 --> 00:10:06,825
that the labour behind that wealth
was questioned even at the time.

161
00:10:06,825 --> 00:10:11,145
What he left in Singapore is
mostly what his money paid for,

162
00:10:11,225 --> 00:10:14,525
a tomb, a school and a
trust that still runs.
